%n [amh]urka | šamaš || aššu (|) aqrati | napištī[ya]
#note: @bib{RN117@846}: “The phrase ‘precious life’ recurs in a prayer to Marduk, @akk{nap-šat UG₃-MEŠ a-qar-t[u]} (@bib{RN403@65, 1}), in comparison with which @i{aššu aqarti napištīya} might have been expected here; on the reversal of nouns and adjectives see Chapter 9, the section on Language and style sub (iii, iv). MS g [BabNB1c]’s spelling of the last word of the phrase as @akk{ZI-ti₃} allows for an analysis of @akk{aq-ra-ti} in both MSS as stative, i.e. @i{aššu aqrat napištī}, ‘because my life is precious’. But when considered in the light of the same manuscript’s @akk{ib-ri-i} for @i{ibrīya} in l. 95, the spelling @akk{ZI-ti₃} might also stand for @i{napištīya}. The adjectival form @i{aqratu} instead of @i{aqartu} can be explained as elevated style: see above, Chapter 9, the section on Language and style sub (i).”
// cf. L I.4 MB "Ur" 4

%n [ē tu]šbī | ina kussî | ša ardāti
#note: See @bib{fromebllab0039@106-107}: ““May you not sit on the young women’s chair!” Previous attempts at restoring the parallel line in the MB Ur MS (UET 6/2 394) include @akk{E₂.EŠ₃.DAM]} (@bib{RN1419Gadd@109}) @i{bīt} @akk{qe₂#-re#-et#} @i{ardāti} (@bib{lambert1992prostitution@129}), and @akk{ma#-aš#-<ta>-ki# ša#} @i{ardāti} (?) (@bib{RN117@302}). The SB parallel suggests reading the Ur tablet as @akk{{giš#}GU#.[Z]A ša#}, which seems compatible with George’s copy (@bib{RN117@pl. 22}), as well as with the digital photograph (@url{http://www.ur-online.org/subject/55645/}).”
// L I.4 MB "Ur" 15

%n ... (|) sūni | ... [...]
#note: @bib{RN117@847–848}: “The first word was read @akk{ne₂#-reb#} by Landsberger (@bib{RN2652@126}), but the space available is not adequate for @akk{ni}; nor do the traces of me first sign permit @akk{u₂#-lap# su-ni}, ‘sanitary towel’ (so @bib{lambert1992prostitution@129}). In my view the first sign; must be @akk{A} or @akk{ZA}; perhaps read @akk{a-kal}? Cf. Pettinato’s rendering of this and the following line: ‘colui che penetra la (tua) vulva possa prendere la sifilide, la sifilide che alberga nella tua vulva possa essere il suo dono’, but this remains highly speculative. As for the word that follows, while I read the adjective in MB Ur as @akk{šu-uh-hu-u₂} (so also A. Westenholz’s copy, against Lambert’s @akk{ša-ah-hu-u₂}; Gadd also saw @akk{ša}), one cannot discount the possibility that there existed a *@i{parras}-stem adjective @i{šaḫḫû}, so here and in the following line there is no need take refuge in the rare value @akk{ŠAH} = @akk{šuh₂}. The couplet 128-9 is a fuller version of MB Ur 38: [x x @i{sūn}]@i{i šuḫḫû lū nidin} x x.”
// L I.4 MB "Ur" 38

